tes there are one or more sensory drivers that are lowering liking, so targeted optimization is likely to move the product toward “high liking” territory.	Use the penalty analysis to pinpoint the specific attributes with the largest negative impact on overall liking, then run focused reformulation to mitigate those drivers (e.g., adjust intensity and/or balance of the most penalizing taste/flavor, aroma notes, sweetness/acidity level, salt, bitterness/astringency, and/or off-notes). Validate that changes do not introduce new penalties by conducting a small follow-up study (n≥50) using the same attribute set plus a check-all-that-apply for any sensory defects (e.g., lingering bitterness, metallic/chemical notes, underdeveloped flavor, texture dryness). Consider iterative optimization with a constrained formulation matrix around the current formulation (not large recipe changes), and include process controls for key variables tied to the penalized attributes (fermentation/aging time, heating/thermal treatment, extraction ratios, particle size/viscosity targets, and aroma retention) to improve repeatability.	CONTINUE_REFINEMENT	2026-04-20 23:56:22.728	2026-04-21 04:34:50.651
